Q1: What is the difference between git add and git commit?

My answer: git add moves your changed files into the staging area — you are telling Git "include these changes in the next snapshot." git commit takes everything in the staging area and permanently saves it as a checkpoint in your local Git history with a label. Add prepares. Commit saves. Neither of these touches GitHub — that is what push is for.


Q2: What is the difference between git push and git pull?

My answer: git push sends your local commits up to GitHub. git pull brings the latest commits from GitHub down to your Codespace. Push is you talking to GitHub. Pull is GitHub talking to you. Do pull first thing every morning before writing any code. Do push when you want your work saved on GitHub.


Q3: What is a branch and why does it exist?

My answer: A branch is an independent copy of the codebase where you can make changes without affecting main. It exists so that incomplete or experimental work does not break the shared codebase that everyone else depends on. You branch, you work, you PR, you merge, you delete. Main stays clean and deployable at all times.


Q4: Walk through what happens when you resolve a merge conflict.

My answer: A conflict happens when two branches changed the same lines of the same file. Git cannot decide which version to keep so it marks both in the file with <<<<<<< HEAD, =======, and >>>>>>> markers. You open the file, read both versions, write the correct final version by hand, delete all three marker lines. Then git add the resolved file and git commit to complete the merge. The commit message is auto-filled by Git.


Q5: What is a Pull Request and why use one instead of pushing straight to main?

My answer: A PR is a proposal to merge one branch into another. It creates a review checkpoint — a teammate reads your code, leaves comments, and approves before anything reaches main. It also triggers CI checks so broken tests block the merge automatically. Pushing straight to main skips all of this — no review, no CI check, broken code can reach everyone immediately.


Q6: What does git stash do and when would you use it?

My answer: git stash saves your uncommitted changes to a temporary stack and reverts your working directory to the last commit — without making a commit. You use it when you need to switch branches urgently but your current work is not ready to commit. git stash pop brings the saved work back.


Q7: What is the difference between git merge and git rebase?

My answer: git merge combines two branches and creates a merge commit — the full history of both branches is preserved including when they diverged. git rebase replays your commits on top of the target branch creating a clean linear history as if you had branched later. Rebase rewrites commit hashes so it must never be used on shared branches that others have already pulled.


Q8: What goes in .gitignore for a Python test automation project and why?

My answer: __pycache__/ and *.pyc because Python generates these automatically and they are machine-specific. venv/ and .venv/ because virtual environments are hundreds of megabytes and can be recreated from requirements.txt. .pytest_cache/ and test reports because they are generated artifacts not source code. .env and secrets.yml because credentials committed to a repo are permanent and readable forever even after deletion.


